title: Text without WPF
intro: Griff draws game text itself now, the same in the editor, an exported game and a browser.
project: Arcade | Glyphs

chapter: The same text everywhere | Fonts read and drawn in plain .NET
say: Every label in this menu, the title, the best score and the buttons, is drawn by Griff itself now, not by Windows.
explain: scene | Scene view | Griff reads the font and rasterizes its letters in plain dot net, so the browser can draw them too.
say: The built-in font is Selawik Semibold, Microsoft's open font with Segoe UI's measurements, so text sits where it always did.
play
wait: 1.5
explain: game | Game | The game draws each letter as its own little quad, from a page of letters drawn once.
stop

chapter: Symbols and fonts | What a font hasn't got comes from the fallback
select: Best
set: Text.Value = Lives ♥♥♥ → Ωmega
expectvalue: Best.Text.Value = Lives ♥♥♥ → Ωmega
explain: scene | Scene view | Hearts, arrows, Greek and Cyrillic come from a part of DejaVu Sans when the font has none.
set: Text.Font = Consolas
expectvalue: Best.Text.Font = Consolas
explain: field:Text.Font | Font | An installed font still works on your own computer, but it's never copied into a game. In a browser it falls back to the built-in font, and the web export warns of it.
set: Text.Font =
say: A font file in Assets ships with the game and works everywhere, so it's the one to choose.

chapter: Scores that change | No garbage
say: Text whose words change every frame, a score or a timer, now makes no garbage: its letters are already on the page.
play
wait: 1
tap: Enter
wait: 2.5
stop
say: And every exported game, desktop or web, carries a third party notices file with the licenses of the fonts and libraries inside it.
